# service — every Home Assistant service hactl can be asked to call is classified
# by how its call reaches entities (D-184):
#
#   target-only  the generic reach covers it: its target, and the entity ids its
#                data names (D-59, D-73, D-106) — exempt here, with its fields
#                listed so a reviewer sees what was judged;
#   mapped       a field names entities by another name (an object id, a domain,
#                a glob, a statistic id) or the service acts on its own entities
#                unnamed — reqmap.mappedServices, or the script and
#                homeassistant fan-out mapped in service.go — proven by its
#                mapping's test;
#   opaque       no field states what the call reaches (a sentence, a shell
#                command, a script body) — refused while the profile denies
#                anything unless allowed by its exact name
#                (enforce.OpaqueService), proven by that test.
#
# Sites are the inventory read off the test instances
# (testdata/policy/services/inventory.json, kept true by
# TestServiceInventoryMatchesHomeAssistant and
# TestE2EServiceInventoryMatchesHomeAssistant) plus every service the code
# maps or treats as opaque. TestServiceSurfaceIsClosed holds the manifest and
# the code's class in lockstep. Fields of custom-integration services are a
# documented limit: only the services listed here were classified.
#ceiling 0

ai_task.clear_images = exempt: target-only — no target; fields: days — none names an entity by another name
ai_task.evaluate = proven: TestOpaqueServiceNeedsAnExactAllowWhileAnythingIsDenied
ai_task.generate_data = proven: TestOpaqueServiceNeedsAnExactAllowWhileAnythingIsDenied
ai_task.generate_image = proven: TestOpaqueServiceNeedsAnExactAllowWhileAnythingIsDenied
